INT. REQUISITIONED OPERA HOUSE - FIELD HEADQUARTERS - DAY

Gilded boxes, a chandelier wrapped in burlap, a stage covered with maps. Plaster cherubs peer down at a long table of officers. Somewhere, a piano is out of tune.

MAJOR PATRICK HALLORAN, 50s, silver at the temples, stands at the center of the stage with a pointer. CAPTAIN LUCIEN BEAUMONT, 40s, a trim moustache and a brigade tie, sits stiffly at his left. LIEUTENANT THOMAS DUPREE, 20s, pale, writes everything down. SERGEANT GRISWOLD, 40s, a boxer's nose, leans on a velvet curtain with arms crossed.

HALLORAN
We hold the bridge until dawn. After that, it isn't ours.

BEAUMONT
He'll never hold until dawn, Major.

DUPREE
Which he, sir?

HALLORAN
(to Dupree)
He means me.

BEAUMONT
(to Halloran)
I mean the man you've put at the east end.

GRISWOLD
He's mine, sir. He's the best I've got.

BEAUMONT
Then God help the rest.

The door at the back bangs open. A scrap of daylight cuts the aisle.

PRIVATE WIGGINS, 18, a mud-streaked messenger with a satchel, runs down the carpet at full tilt and salutes, panting.

WIGGINS
Major, sir. The bridge is gone.

Silence. A cherub's plaster smile hangs over them.

HALLORAN
How gone?

WIGGINS
Blown, sir. From our side.

Beaumont slowly stands. Halloran lowers his pointer.
